{0 Blocking Linux tmux clients}

{!Libtmux_unix.Client} attaches to an explicit existing endpoint and returns
immutable snapshots. Clients require Linux pidfds, the daemon's PID namespace
and a shared local scratch filesystem. Closing a client leaves the daemon alive.

{1 Cancellation and retained outcomes}

Retain the handle from {!Libtmux_unix.Client.submit}, await it, then inspect
{!Libtmux_unix.Operation} after success, failure or cancellation. Scoped
{!Libtmux_unix.Cancellation} tokens can be signalled from another domain.
Written requests drain under bounded ownership; cancellation does not undo them.

{1 Output and workspaces}

{!Libtmux_unix.Subscription} separates future bytes from rendered-screen waits.
Overflow and coverage changes produce explicit gaps. The blocking backend
advances stream lanes while waiting.

{!Libtmux_unix.Workspace} executes validated ephemeral plans and retains partial
journals. Compensation targets proven newly created panes and preserves foreign
resources. Cleanup failures have separate outcomes. An existing client remains
caller-owned; a bootstrap handle owns its new client, without owning the daemon.
